    "summary": "Learn the parts of a computer in plain language - screen, keyboard, CPU, memory, storage - and what they mean when you buy or use a PC.",
    "content": "# Lesson 01: Meet Your Computer: The Main Parts and What Each One Does\n\nBefore you can use a computer with confidence, it helps to know what is inside it and what each part does. You do not need to be a technician; you just need a simple picture in your head. When someone says *\"your computer is slow because you have little RAM\"* or *\"your disk is full\"*, you will know exactly what they mean.\n\n## 1. The big picture\nEvery computer, whether it is a desktop, a laptop, a tablet or even your phone, does four jobs:\n\n| Job | What it means | Part that does it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Input** | You give the computer information | Keyboard, mouse, touchpad, microphone, camera |\n| **Processing** | The computer \"thinks\" and calculates | Processor (CPU) |\n| **Storage** | The computer keeps your files | Hard drive or SSD |\n| **Output** | The computer shows you the result | Screen, speakers, printer |\n\n## 2. The main parts explained simply\n- **Processor (CPU)** - the \"brain\". It follows instructions very fast. Names you will see: *Intel Core i5 / i7*, *AMD Ryzen 5 / 7*, *Apple M2 / M3*. A higher number usually means faster.\n- **Memory (RAM)** - the computer's *desk*. Programs you are using right now sit here. More RAM means you can open more programs at the same time without slowing down. **8 GB** is the minimum for daily work today; **16 GB** is comfortable.\n- **Storage (SSD or HDD)** - the computer's *cupboard*. Your files, photos and programs are kept here even when the computer is off. An **SSD** is much faster than an old **HDD**. 256 GB is small, 512 GB is good for most people.\n- **Screen (display)** - where you see everything. Size is measured diagonally in inches (for example 14\" or 15.6\").\n- **Battery** (laptops) - how long you can work without plugging in.\n- **Ports** - the holes on the side: **USB-A** (the classic rectangle), **USB-C** (small and oval), **HDMI** (for a TV or projector) and the headphone jack.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Easy way to remember**: RAM is your desk (work you are doing *now*), storage is your cupboard (things you *keep*). A bigger desk lets you work on more things at once; a bigger cupboard lets you keep more things.\n\n## 3. Worked examples\n### Example 1: Reading a laptop advert\n*\"Laptop 15.6\", Intel Core i5, 16 GB RAM, 512 GB SSD, Windows 11\"*\n- 15.6\" - the screen size.\n- Core i5 - a good mid-range processor.\n- 16 GB RAM - can run many programs and browser tabs smoothly.\n- 512 GB SSD - fast storage with enough space for documents and photos.\n\n### Example 2: Finding out what YOUR computer has (Windows 11)\n1. Press **Windows key + I** to open **Settings**.\n2. Click **System**, then scroll down and click **About**.\n3. Look at **Processor**, **Installed RAM** and **System type**.\n4. For storage, open **Settings > System > Storage** to see how much space is used and free.\n\n### Example 3: On a Mac\nClick the **Apple menu** in the top-left corner and choose **About This Mac**. You will see the chip (processor), memory (RAM) and macOS version. Click **More Info > Storage** to see free space.\n\n## 4. Common mistakes\n- **Confusing RAM with storage.** \"I have 512 GB of memory\" usually means storage, not RAM.\n- **Buying only by price.** A cheap laptop with 4 GB RAM and an HDD will feel slow from day one.\n- **Ignoring the ports.** If you use a projector or a USB flash drive often, make sure the laptop has HDMI and USB-A, or buy an adapter.\n\n## 5. Hands-on Student Challenge\nFind the specifications of the computer you are using right now and write them in a small table:\n1. Processor name\n2. Installed RAM\n3. Total storage and free storage\n4. Windows (or macOS) version\n5. Then decide: is this computer *good*, *okay*, or *needs an upgrade* for office work with many browser tabs?\n\n:::solution\n**How to find each item on Windows 11:**\n\n| Item | Where to find it | Example result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Processor | Settings > System > About > *Processor* | Intel Core i5-1235U |\n| Installed RAM | Settings > System > About > *Installed RAM* | 16.0 GB |\n| Storage | Settings > System > Storage | 512 GB total, 210 GB free |\n| Windows version | Settings > System > About > *Windows specifications* | Windows 11 Pro 23H2 |\n\n**How to judge it:**\n- RAM **8 GB or more** and an **SSD** -> good for office work.\n- RAM **4 GB**, or an **HDD**, or less than **20 GB free** -> it will feel slow; consider freeing space or upgrading.\n\nYour numbers will be different - what matters is that you found all four values yourself.\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- A computer does four jobs: input, processing, storage and output.\n- CPU = brain, RAM = desk (current work), SSD/HDD = cupboard (saved files).\n- You can always check your computer's specs in **Settings > System > About**.\n",
