    "title": "Lesson 02: Turning On, Sleep, Restart and Shut Down - Doing It the Right Way",
    "slug": "it-lesson-02-turning-on-sleep-restart-and-shut-down-doing-it-the-right-way",
    "category": "computer_skills",
    "track": "it_skills",
    "track_name": "Computer & IT Skills",
    "category_name": "Computer & IT Skills",
    "difficulty": "Beginner",
    "summary": "Understand the difference between sleep, restart and shut down, when to use each one, and how to handle a computer that will not turn off.",
    "content": "# Lesson 02: Turning On, Sleep, Restart and Shut Down - Doing It the Right Way\n\nIt sounds simple, but many computer problems come from turning the computer off the wrong way, or from never restarting it at all. In this lesson you will learn the four power options, when each one is the right choice, and what to do when the computer freezes.\n\n## 1. The four power options\n\n| Option | What happens | When to use it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Sleep** | Screen turns off, your open work stays in memory, wakes up in 2 seconds | Short breaks: lunch, a meeting |\n| **Shut down** | Everything closes, the computer turns fully off | End of the day, before travelling, before opening the case |\n| **Restart** | Shuts down and starts again automatically | After updates, when something is acting strange |\n| **Lock** | Your work stays open but a password is needed to come back | Every time you walk away from the desk |\n\n**Where to find them (Windows 11):** click **Start**, then the **Power** icon at the bottom right of the Start menu.\n\n## 2. Why restarting fixes so many problems\nWhile the computer runs, programs fill the memory, small errors pile up, and updates wait to be installed. A **restart** clears the memory and starts everything fresh. That is why IT support always asks: *\"Have you tried restarting it?\"* - it really does solve a large number of problems.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Restart your computer **at least once a week**. Simply closing the laptop lid every day for weeks puts it to *sleep*, not off, and problems slowly build up.\n\n## 3. Worked examples\n### Example 1: Locking your screen in one second\nPress **Windows key + L**. Your screen locks immediately, and nobody can read your email while you are getting coffee. On a Mac use **Control + Command + Q**.\n\n### Example 2: Shutting down using the keyboard only\n1. Press **Windows key + X** to open the quick menu.\n2. Press **U**, then **U** again to shut down (or **R** to restart).\n\nYou can also go to the desktop (**Windows key + D**) and press **Alt + F4**, then choose *Shut down* from the list.\n\n### Example 3: The computer is frozen and will not respond\n1. Wait 30 seconds - it may be busy.\n2. Press **Ctrl + Alt + Delete**. If a blue menu appears, click the **Power** icon in the bottom right and choose **Restart**.\n3. Only if nothing responds: hold the **power button for about 10 seconds** until it turns off. Wait a few seconds and turn it on again.\n\nThe forced shutdown in step 3 can lose unsaved work, so use it only as a last resort.\n\n## 4. Common mistakes\n- **Pulling the plug or holding the power button every time.** This can damage files that were being saved.\n- **Never restarting.** Updates stay pending and the computer slowly becomes slower.\n- **Closing the lid and putting the laptop in a bag while it is still running.** It can overheat inside the bag. Shut it down before travelling.\n\n## 5. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. Lock your computer using a keyboard shortcut, then unlock it.\n2. Find out when your computer was last restarted. Hint: open **Task Manager** (**Ctrl + Shift + Esc**), click **Performance**, then **CPU**, and look at **Up time**.\n3. If the up time is more than 3 days, save your work and restart.\n4. Change what happens when you close the laptop lid (search the Start menu for *\"lid\"*).\n\n:::solution\n1. Press **Windows key + L** to lock. Unlock with your PIN, password or face/fingerprint.\n2. **Ctrl + Shift + Esc** opens Task Manager. Go to **Performance > CPU**. *Up time* is shown like `5:03:22:10` = 5 days, 3 hours, 22 minutes.\n3. If it is more than 3 days: save your files, then **Start > Power > Restart**. After the restart, up time starts again from `0:00:00:xx`.\n4. Search Start for **\"Change what closing the lid does\"**. You will see two columns - *On battery* and *Plugged in*. Choose **Sleep** (recommended) or **Do nothing**, then click **Save changes**.\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- Sleep for short breaks, Shut down at the end of the day, Restart when something feels wrong.\n- **Windows key + L** locks your screen instantly - use it every time you leave the desk.\n- Holding the power button is the emergency option only.\n",
