    "summary": "Find your way around Windows 11: the desktop, Start menu, taskbar, system tray and notification area, and learn to pin your favourite apps.",
    "content": "# Lesson 03: The Windows Desktop, Start Menu and Taskbar\n\nThe desktop, the Start menu and the taskbar are the \"home screen\" of Windows. If you know these three areas well, you can open any program, switch between windows and check the time, battery, Wi-Fi and sound without searching around.\n\n## 1. The three main areas\n- **Desktop** - the large background area. You can keep shortcuts here, but try to keep it tidy.\n- **Taskbar** - the bar at the bottom of the screen. It shows open programs and pinned favourites.\n- **Start menu** - opens when you click the **Windows logo** or press the **Windows key**. It has a search box, pinned apps and recommended files.\n\nOn the right side of the taskbar is the **system tray**: Wi-Fi, sound, battery, language (for example *ENG* / *AR*), date and time. Clicking the date opens **notifications** and the **calendar**.\n\n## 2. The fastest way to open anything\nPress the **Windows key** and just **start typing**. You do not need to click in the search box.\n- Type `word` -> press **Enter** to open Word.\n- Type `calc` -> opens Calculator.\n- Type `bluetooth` -> opens Bluetooth settings.\n- Type the name of a file you saved yesterday -> Windows finds it.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Typing in the Start menu is usually faster than looking for icons. Make it a habit: **Windows key, type, Enter.**\n\n## 3. Worked examples\n### Example 1: Pin an app to the taskbar\n1. Open the app (for example Excel).\n2. Right-click its icon on the taskbar.\n3. Click **Pin to taskbar**. Now it stays there even after you close it.\n\nTo remove it: right-click > **Unpin from taskbar**.\n\n### Example 2: Open taskbar apps with the keyboard\nPinned taskbar apps are numbered from left to right. **Windows key + 1** opens the first one, **Windows key + 2** the second, and so on. If Outlook is in position 3, **Windows key + 3** opens it from anywhere.\n\n### Example 3: Move the Start button to the left\nSome people prefer the old Windows look. Right-click an empty part of the taskbar > **Taskbar settings** > **Taskbar behaviours** > **Taskbar alignment** > choose **Left**.\n\n## 4. Common mistakes\n- **Saving everything on the desktop.** The desktop becomes a mess and files are hard to find. Use the Documents folder (Lesson 11).\n- **Too many pinned icons.** Pin only the 5-8 apps you use every day.\n- **Ignoring the system tray.** Many problems (no sound, no internet, wrong keyboard language) can be fixed with one click there.\n\n## 5. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. Pin your three most-used apps to the taskbar and arrange them in the order you like (drag the icons).\n2. Open the second pinned app using only the keyboard.\n3. Open **Settings > Bluetooth** using only the Start menu search.\n4. Check the battery percentage and the current keyboard language from the system tray.\n\n:::solution\n1. Open each app, right-click its taskbar icon > **Pin to taskbar**. Drag icons left/right to reorder.\n2. Press **Windows key + 2** - the second pinned app opens (or comes to the front if it is already open).\n3. Press **Windows key**, type `bluetooth`, press **Enter** - *Bluetooth & devices* settings opens.\n4. Look at the bottom-right corner: the battery icon shows the percentage when you hover over it; the language indicator shows *ENG* or *AR* (click it, or press **Windows key + Space**, to switch).\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- The Start menu search (**Windows key + type**) is the fastest way to open apps, settings and files.\n- Pin daily apps to the taskbar and open them with **Windows key + number**.\n- The system tray holds Wi-Fi, sound, battery, language and notifications.\n",
