    "title": "Lesson 04: Mouse and Touchpad Skills: Click, Double-Click, Right-Click, Drag and Scroll",
    "slug": "it-lesson-04-mouse-and-touchpad-skills-click-double-click-right-click-drag-and-scroll",
    "category": "computer_skills",
    "track": "it_skills",
    "track_name": "Computer & IT Skills",
    "category_name": "Computer & IT Skills",
    "difficulty": "Beginner",
    "summary": "Master every mouse and touchpad action - including right-click menus and multi-finger gestures - so you can work faster and with less effort.",
    "content": "# Lesson 04: Mouse and Touchpad Skills: Click, Double-Click, Right-Click, Drag and Scroll\n\nThe mouse is the tool you will use more than any other. Knowing all of its actions - especially the **right-click** and the touchpad **gestures** - saves many clicks every day.\n\n## 1. The five mouse actions\n\n| Action | How | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Click** | Press the left button once | Selects something or presses a button |\n| **Double-click** | Press the left button twice quickly | Opens a file or folder |\n| **Right-click** | Press the right button once | Shows a menu of options for that item |\n| **Drag** | Hold the left button, move, release | Moves files, selects text, resizes windows |\n| **Scroll** | Roll the wheel | Moves the page up and down |\n\n**Right-click is your best friend.** Not sure what you can do with something? Right-click on it - the menu shows the options.\n\n## 2. Touchpad gestures (Windows 11 laptops)\n\n| Gesture | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Tap with one finger | Click |\n| Tap with two fingers | Right-click |\n| Two fingers up/down | Scroll |\n| Pinch two fingers | Zoom in / out |\n| Three fingers swipe up | Show all open windows |\n| Three fingers swipe down | Show the desktop |\n| Three fingers swipe left/right | Switch between apps |\n\nOn a Mac: two-finger click = right-click, and many of the same swipes work.\n\n## 3. Worked examples\n### Example 1: Selecting text exactly\n- **Double-click** a word to select that word.\n- **Triple-click** inside a paragraph to select the whole paragraph.\n- Click at the start, then **hold Shift and click** at the end to select everything between.\n\n### Example 2: Selecting several files\n- **Ctrl + click** each file to pick files that are not next to each other.\n- Click the first file, then **Shift + click** the last file to select a whole range.\n- Drag a box around files with the mouse to select a group.\n\n### Example 3: Making the pointer easier to see\nOpen **Settings > Accessibility > Mouse pointer and touch**. Increase the size and choose a bright colour. Very useful for presentations and for older eyes.\n\n## 4. Common mistakes\n- **Double-clicking buttons and links.** Buttons and web links need only **one** click. Double-clicking can submit a form twice.\n- **Clicking too slowly for a double-click.** If files will not open, change the speed in *Settings > Bluetooth & devices > Mouse > Additional mouse settings*.\n- **Dragging files by accident.** If a file suddenly disappears, press **Ctrl + Z** straight away to undo the move.\n\n## 5. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. Open any web article. Select one word, then one whole paragraph, using only double- and triple-clicks.\n2. In File Explorer, select files 1, 3 and 5 from a folder (not 2 and 4).\n3. Use a touchpad gesture (or **Windows key + Tab**) to see all open windows.\n4. Make your mouse pointer larger and change its colour.\n\n:::solution\n1. **Double-click** a word -> it is highlighted. **Triple-click** inside the paragraph -> the whole paragraph is highlighted.\n2. Click file 1, then hold **Ctrl** and click file 3 and file 5. Exactly three files are highlighted.\n3. Three-finger swipe **up** on the touchpad, or press **Windows key + Tab**. All open windows appear as thumbnails; click one to switch to it.\n4. **Settings > Accessibility > Mouse pointer and touch** -> drag the *Size* slider to 3 or 4, and choose the *Custom* colour style (for example bright yellow).\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- One click for buttons and links, double-click to open files and folders.\n- Right-click (or two-finger tap) shows what you can do with anything.\n- Ctrl + click picks separate items; Shift + click picks a range.\n",
