    "title": "Lesson 06: Working with Windows: Open, Minimize, Maximize, Resize and Snap",
    "slug": "it-lesson-06-working-with-windows-open-minimize-maximize-resize-and-snap",
    "category": "computer_skills",
    "track": "it_skills",
    "track_name": "Computer & IT Skills",
    "category_name": "Computer & IT Skills",
    "difficulty": "Beginner",
    "summary": "Control program windows like a pro - minimize, maximize, resize, and use Snap to put two or four windows side by side.",
    "content": "# Lesson 06: Working with Windows: Open, Minimize, Maximize, Resize and Snap\n\nEvery program opens in a *window*. When you have email, a report and a website open at the same time, knowing how to arrange windows is the difference between a calm screen and a messy one.\n\n## 1. The parts of a window\nAt the top right of almost every window you will see three buttons:\n- **-** Minimize: hides the window to the taskbar (it is still open).\n- **[ ]** Maximize: makes the window fill the screen (click again to restore).\n- **X** Close: closes the window / program.\n\nThe **title bar** at the top shows the name of the file and program. Drag it to move the window. Drag any **edge or corner** to resize it.\n\n## 2. Snap: two windows side by side\nWindows 11 can arrange windows neatly for you:\n- **Windows key + Left arrow** - the window takes the left half of the screen.\n- **Windows key + Right arrow** - the right half.\n- **Windows key + Up arrow** - maximize.\n- **Windows key + Down arrow** - restore / minimize.\n- Hover over the **Maximize** button to see **Snap layouts** - choose 2, 3 or 4 window positions.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Comparing two documents, copying data from a website into Excel, or writing an email while reading a PDF - all are much easier with **two windows snapped side by side.**\n\n## 3. Worked examples\n### Example 1: Email on the left, report on the right\n1. Click on the email window and press **Windows key + Left arrow**.\n2. Windows shows the other open windows on the right half - click the report.\n3. Both now share the screen 50/50. Drag the line between them to change the sizes.\n\n### Example 2: Switching between windows\n- **Alt + Tab** - hold Alt and keep pressing Tab to move through open windows; release to switch.\n- **Windows key + Tab** - shows all windows as large thumbnails.\n- **Windows key + D** - shows the desktop; press again to bring everything back.\n\n### Example 3: A window opened off the screen\nSometimes a window opens where you cannot see it (often after disconnecting a second monitor). Click its icon on the taskbar, then press **Windows key + Up arrow** to maximize it back onto your screen.\n\n## 4. Common mistakes\n- **Closing instead of minimizing.** The **X** closes the program; unsaved work may be lost. Use **-** to hide it.\n- **Opening the same program many times.** Check the taskbar first - a line under the icon means it is already open.\n- **Working with tiny windows.** Use Snap instead of dragging edges by hand.\n\n## 5. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. Open a web browser and File Explorer.\n2. Snap the browser to the left half and File Explorer to the right half using only the keyboard.\n3. Open a third app (Calculator) and use **Snap layouts** to place three windows on the screen.\n4. Show the desktop with one shortcut, then bring all windows back.\n\n:::solution\n1. **Windows key**, type `edge` (or `chrome`) > **Enter**; then **Windows key + E** for File Explorer.\n2. Click the browser > **Windows key + Left arrow**. Click File Explorer > **Windows key + Right arrow**. (Or choose File Explorer from the thumbnails Windows offers on the right side.)\n3. Open Calculator (**Windows key**, type `calc`, **Enter**). Hover the mouse over the Maximize button of any window (or press **Windows key + Z**) and pick a three-window layout. Click a window for each empty zone.\n4. **Windows key + D** shows the desktop; press **Windows key + D** again and all windows return.\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- Minimize hides, Maximize fills the screen, X closes.\n- **Windows key + arrows** and **Snap layouts** arrange windows instantly.\n- **Alt + Tab** switches windows; **Windows key + D** shows the desktop.\n",
    "tags": "IT Skills, Lesson 06, Windows Basics, Beginner",
