    "id": "it-97",
    "lesson_number": 97,
    "title": "Lesson 97: Using AI Assistants Wisely: ChatGPT, Copilot, Claude and Gemini at Work",
    "slug": "it-lesson-97-using-ai-assistants-wisely-chatgpt-copilot-claude-and-gemini-at-work",
    "category": "computer_skills",
    "track": "it_skills",
    "track_name": "Computer & IT Skills",
    "category_name": "Computer & IT Skills",
    "difficulty": "Beginner",
    "summary": "Use AI chat assistants to draft emails, summarise documents, explain errors and create formulas - while protecting confidential data and checking the answers.",
    "content": "# Lesson 97: Using AI Assistants Wisely: ChatGPT, Copilot, Claude and Gemini at Work\n\nAI assistants such as **Microsoft Copilot**, **ChatGPT**, **Claude** and **Google Gemini** can save hours every week: drafting, summarising, explaining and brainstorming. Used carelessly, they can also leak confidential data or give confident wrong answers.\n\n## 1. What AI assistants are good at\n- **Drafting**: emails, announcements, outlines, job descriptions.\n- **Rewriting**: shorter, more polite, more formal, translated.\n- **Summarising**: long emails, articles, meeting notes.\n- **Explaining**: an Excel formula, an error message, a technical term.\n- **Brainstorming**: ideas for a training session or a campaign.\n\n## 2. How to ask (prompting)\nA good request includes: **role/context + task + details + format**.\n> *\"I'm a sales manager. Write a short, polite email to a pharmacy owner reminding him of our meeting on Tuesday at 10 AM about the new display stand. Maximum 80 words, professional tone.\"*\n\nThen refine: *\"Make it warmer\"*, *\"Add an Arabic version\"*, *\"Shorter\"*.\n\n## 3. Rules for safe use\n1. **Follow your company policy** - many companies allow only an approved tool (e.g. Copilot with work account).\n2. **Don't paste confidential data** (customer lists, prices, personal data, passwords) into public AI tools.\n3. **Check facts, numbers and names** - AI can be confidently wrong (\"hallucinate\").\n4. **You are responsible** for anything you send.\n\n## 4. Worked examples\n### Example 1: Explaining an Excel formula\nPrompt: *\"Explain this Excel formula step by step in simple words: `=IFERROR(XLOOKUP(A2,Products!A:A,Products!C:C),\"Not found\")`\"*. The assistant explains each part - then try it yourself.\n\n### Example 2: Summarising a long email thread\nPrompt: *\"Summarise this email thread in 5 bullet points and list any actions with owners and deadlines:\"* + paste the text (after removing confidential details if using a public tool).\n\n### Example 3: Copilot in Microsoft 365 (if your company has it)\nIn Word: *Draft with Copilot*; in Outlook: *Summarise* a thread; in Teams: *Recap* a meeting. These use your work data under your company's protection.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Treat AI like a fast, helpful intern: great first drafts, but always review before it goes out.\n\n## 5. Common mistakes\n- **Copy-pasting AI output without reading it.**\n- **Uploading confidential files to personal AI accounts.**\n- **Vague prompts** like \"write an email\" - you get a generic result.\n\n## 6. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. Write a detailed prompt asking an AI assistant to draft a thank-you email after a customer visit (include role, details, length and tone).\n2. Ask it to rewrite the email in Arabic.\n3. List three things you must check before sending it.\n\n:::solution\n1. Example prompt:\n> *\"I'm an Area Sales Manager for an eye-care company. Write a thank-you email to Dr. Khalid, a pharmacy owner in Jeddah, after my visit today. Thank him for his time, confirm we will deliver the new shelf display next Sunday, and offer to train his staff. Professional and friendly tone, maximum 100 words.\"*\n2. Follow-up prompt: *\"Now write the same email in formal Arabic.\"*\n3. Check: (a) **names, dates and promises** are correct (delivery date, product names); (b) **no invented details** (prices, discounts you never offered); (c) **tone and language** fit the customer, and it follows company rules. Also remove any confidential data before using a public tool.\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- AI assistants are great for drafting, rewriting, summarising and explaining.\n- Give context, task, details and format in your prompt.\n- Protect confidential data and always check the output.\n",
    "tags": "IT Skills, Lesson 97, Productivity, Beginner",
